# Coordinating language features in the editor

Projection, transport, reply mapping, diagnostics, and safe actions each have a
clear local contract. The editor still needs one coordinator to make those contracts
hold together while documents, focus, settings, processes, and background work
change over time.

The coordinator does not create a second semantic model. It asks the editor-side
repository for a captured disk-and-buffer snapshot, projects that snapshot through
the pure engine, and routes requests through the session and mapping boundaries from
the preceding chapters. Its main responsibility is temporal: only work belonging to
the current editor situation may become visible.

## Eligibility is checked before expensive work

Providers are registered for local Markdown documents whose paths end in
`.literate`. Runtime guards then require an open document in its owning local
workspace folder, workspace trust, and a configured canonical language server for
the projected output.

Executable configuration comes only from inspected global settings. Fence aliases
may normalize a fragment's language label, but aliases are not accepted as server
configuration keys. This keeps executable authority outside the workspace while
still letting familiar fence labels select a canonical language.

A cheap single-buffer parse answers one early question: is the cursor inside the
body of a named fragment rather than prose, a fence line, a fragment reference, or
an unfinished reference marker? This local check can enable prompt cancellation of
a pending automatic request. It does not prove that a projection exists. Every real
request still captures and validates the complete project.

## One foreground operation owns the asynchronous path

Interactive operations share a serialization gate. A request moves through snapshot
capture, projection, session initialization, capability checking, synchronization,
protocol request, reply validation, and mapping as one owned operation. The waiting
queue is bounded, and foreground work takes precedence over background diagnostics.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Provider as Editor provider
    participant Gate as Foreground gate
    participant Repo as Project repository
    participant Projector
    participant Session
    participant Mapper

    Provider->>Gate: Request with cancellation and editor revision
    Gate->>Repo: Capture disk and open-buffer snapshot
    Repo-->>Gate: Documents and templates
    Gate->>Projector: Build complete projections
    Projector-->>Gate: Files, mappings, or diagnostics
    Gate->>Session: Initialize and synchronize language family
    Gate->>Session: Capability-gated request
    Session-->>Gate: Untrusted reply
    Gate->>Mapper: Validate against captured occurrence
    Mapper-->>Provider: Fresh mapped result or no result
```

Each return to the gate is an asynchronous boundary. After every such boundary the
coordinator rechecks cancellation, document revision, source-input revision,
workspace membership, trust, configuration, and any automatic-typing candidate.
The diagram's final “no result” is normal defensive behavior, not necessarily an
error.

The captured snapshot must include the exact text of every open local literate
buffer. If a buffer and snapshot disagree, the request is abandoned and old
language diagnostics for that folder are cleared. Source templates are captured as
inputs too, including unsaved template buffers supplied by the repository.

## Sessions are shared without sharing failures

A session key combines workspace folder and canonical language. Requests in that
scope reuse one process. JavaScript and TypeScript family outputs are synchronized
together; an unrelated configured language receives its own session and document
set.

A stopped or failed session clears its diagnostic ownership and remains disabled.
The coordinator reports one bounded, categorized message rather than repeatedly
launching the same broken process. Language-server configuration changes and the
restart command reset sessions, snapshots, diagnostic ownership, and reported-failure
state.

Messages in the language-server output channel avoid source text, configuration
values, raw protocol payloads, and raw failures. They identify safe categories such
as a missing executable, permission failure, timeout, unsupported synchronization,
position-encoding disagreement, invalid protocol data, or a projection problem.
The channel itself retains only a bounded recent history.

## Automatic completion keeps only the latest insertion

Automatic behavior should feel like editing code, not like enabling suggestions for
all Markdown. Literate observes a single-character insertion in the focused editor,
with one empty selection, and checks that the resulting cursor remains in a local
named-fragment body. Replacements, multiline insertions, multi-character paste,
undo or redo, and multiple cursors do not start automatic work. The editor API cannot
distinguish a one-character paste from typing, so either can qualify.

The candidate waits for a short debounce. Further eligible typing replaces it; a
busy initialization or hover retains only the newest candidate rather than building
a request queue. Identifier characters can request completion. Punctuation does so
only when the selected server advertises it as a completion trigger. Parenthesis-like
characters may instead request signature help when that server's capability selects
them, using a small conservative default when it omits an explicit trigger list.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Idle
    Idle --> Pending: eligible one-character insertion
    Pending --> Pending: newer eligible insertion replaces candidate
    Pending --> Cancelled: cursor, editor, focus, non-eligible input, source, or setting changes
    Pending --> Requesting: debounce ends and gate is free
    Requesting --> Cancelled: freshness or cancellation fails
    Requesting --> Cached: nonempty validated result
    Cached --> Idle: matching editor provider consumes once
    Cancelled --> Idle
```

The state diagram intentionally combines completion and signature prefetch because
they share the same freshness machinery. A cached result is consumed once by the
normal editor provider; it is not a second source of truth.

Escape cancellation is enabled as soon as the local fragment-body check succeeds,
before snapshot capture or server startup. It cancels only pending automatic work.
Once the normal suggestion interface owns the interaction, its ordinary Escape
behavior remains untouched. Selection, active editor, focus, trust, document, input,
or configuration changes clear stale candidates.

Automatic triggering is independently configurable per resource. Manual provider
invocation continues to use the same projection and reply guards.

## Signature triggers belong to their selected server

The coordinator starts with a small conservative trigger set and learns bounded
trigger and retrigger characters from initialized servers. Provider registration may
contain the union needed by the editor API, but that union is not routing authority.
Before requesting help, Literate asks whether the server selected by the current
projection actually advertised the character.

A retrigger-only character requires an existing signature-help interaction. A
character learned from another language session cannot steal a completion request in
the current one. This distinction matters in a shared Markdown document where
several fragment languages may coexist.

## Background diagnostics use a different freshness clock

Diagnostics should update even when the editor window is not focused. Opening or
editing an eligible document schedules one latest background candidate per workspace
folder after its own debounce. Background work captures the project, projects it,
starts only configured output-language sessions, and synchronizes their documents
through the diagnostics owner.

Focus and selection changes invalidate interactive requests but do not by themselves
make a diagnostic snapshot stale. Source or template changes do. Keeping separate
editor-interaction and source-input revisions prevents moving the cursor from erasing
valid Problems while still stopping a hover intended for an old position.

Background work never competes indefinitely with typing. It starts only while the
shared gate is free and yields when a foreground candidate or waiter appears. It
performs synchronization and lets the diagnostics controller own later push or pull
reports; it does not issue hover or completion requests.

## Invalidation follows semantic inputs

Changes to a `.literate` file or a captured template invalidate source freshness,
clear language diagnostics, cancel pending work, and schedule new background capture
where appropriate. Workspace-folder, trust, and language-server configuration
changes reset broader session state.

During snapshot capture, filesystem events are recorded under a bound. Once template
paths are known, a change to one of those paths invalidates the capture. Events for
generated source, publication output, or transaction staging files do not by
themselves cancel a cold request; those paths are results, not captured language
inputs. A newer editor edit or cursor move still invalidates the request normally.

This distinction prevents ordinary generation activity from continually cancelling
language intelligence while ensuring that a newly discovered template cannot change
under an in-flight projection unnoticed.

## Providers share the pipeline, not unchecked replies

Hover, completion, signature help, navigation, diagnostics, and explicit safe actions
all reuse snapshot capture, projection, session selection, synchronization, and
freshness checks. Their reply validators remain separate because each feature has a
different safe result shape.

Existing fragment-oriented editor features are also separate. A fragment reference
inside Markdown is answered by the fragment model, while a symbol inside projected
program text is answered by the configured language server. The local body guard and
projection mapping keep those contexts from competing.

Across the complete coordinator, no language request saves a document, generates an
output, or directly applies a workspace change. The editor receives bounded display
data, navigation locations, completion insertions, diagnostics, or a validated
action for the user to choose. That closes the language-intelligence part of the
book: assembled code can use ordinary language tools while the literate source
remains authoritative.
